WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.568
Putting your site on your own web address.

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.587
It takes about five minutes of your time.

00:00:05.987 --> 00:00:10.621
First, the part that worries people. You are not transferring anything.

00:00:10.621 --> 00:00:16.627
Your domain stays exactly where you bought it, in your name, with nothing unlocked or moved.

00:00:16.627 --> 00:00:24.330
Your email is not affected, because the setting that carries email is separate from the one that carries your website.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:29.160
Your old address keeps working while you switch, and it can all be undone.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.167
Step one. Type your address into the box on your dashboard, and press Save.

00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:39.755
Just the address itself, with no https in front.

00:00:41.155 --> 00:00:44.335
Step two, and this is the only fiddly bit.

00:00:44.335 --> 00:00:49.860
Sign in to whoever you bought the domain from, and find the DNS settings.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.072
Add two records.

00:00:51.072 --> 00:00:59.020
Both are type A, and both point at the same IP address, which is on the guide page just below this video.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.562
Name the first one @, which simply means the address itself.

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:05.378
Name the second one www.

00:01:05.378 --> 00:01:11.434
If records already exist for either, change those rather than adding duplicates.

00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:24.869
If you are not sure where the DNS settings live, the page below lists the exact screens for GoDaddy, Namecheap, IONOS, Squarespace, Wix and Cloudflare.

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:29.970
If yours is not there, reply and tell us who you bought it from.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:33.798
Step three is that your part is done.

00:01:33.798 --> 00:01:36.684
We watch for the change and handle the rest:

00:01:36.684 --> 00:01:42.654
the secure padlock, telling search engines the right address, and email on your own domain.

00:01:42.654 --> 00:01:45.999
Your dashboard tells you the moment it is finished.

00:01:47.399 --> 00:01:52.670
If you already have a website at that address, make the change at a quiet time of day.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:58.002
While it spreads, some visitors reach the old site and some the new one, and both work.

00:01:58.002 --> 00:02:03.885
There is a brief window while the certificate is issued, which is why evening beats mid morning.

00:02:05.285 --> 00:02:06.881
That is the whole job.

00:02:06.881 --> 00:02:16.525
If anything looks stuck, it is usually that only one of the two records was added, or your provider has not published the change yet.

00:02:16.525 --> 00:02:20.876
Neither breaks anything. Still unfinished after a few hours?

00:02:20.876 --> 00:02:22.979
Reply to us and we will look.
